text:
On 22 September 1892, it has been exactly a hundred years since the First Republic was proclaimed. The Third Republic organises big celebrations for this [centenary].

In Paris, the President of the Republic, Sadi Carnot, leads a great ceremony at the Panthéon, the monument where the nation honours its great figures. There are parades, speeches and illuminations. All over France, towns decorate their streets with tricolour flags and hold banquets and dances.

The celebration is a chance to look back and tell the story of the Republic since the Revolution:
- 1789: the Declaration of the Rights of Man;
- 1792: the First Republic;
- 1830: the Three Glorious Days and the return of the tricolour flag;
- 1848: the Second Republic, universal male suffrage and the abolition of slavery;
- 1870: the Third Republic.

In a hundred years, France has had kings, emperors and republics. But in 1892, the Republic finally seems here to stay.

All the symbols of the Republic are now in place: the tricolour flag, Marianne, the motto "Liberty, Equality, Fraternity", La Marseillaise and 14 July. They are the same as today!

And children learn to love the Republic... at school. That is the republicans' great project.

To run all these new schools, thousands of men and women teachers are needed.

From 1879, every département must have a [teacher training college] for men and one for women. Future teachers are trained there for three years. They start at about 16, often from modest families of farmers or craftsmen.

Teachers often wear a long black frock coat. The writer Charles Péguy nicknamed them the "[black hussars] of the Republic": like soldiers, their mission is to spread the ideas of the Republic across the country.

The teacher is a very important person in the village. He is often also the town hall secretary. He is respected, like the mayor and the priest.

Teachers teach children to read, write and count, but also:
- to speak French. At the time, many children first speak a regional language at home: Breton, Occitan, Basque, Alsatian, Corsican... At school, speaking them is often forbidden, and children who do can be punished;
- to know France, its history and geography;
- to love the Republic and the homeland;
- to respect good morals: honesty, hard work, politeness, hygiene.

Women teachers also play a big role, especially in girls' schools. For many young women, becoming a teacher is one of the few jobs that lets them be independent.

After the war, the French want to rebuild the country and create a fairer Republic. In 1946, they adopt a new Constitution by [referendum]: this is the Fourth Republic.

At the start of this Constitution, a text called the [preamble] adds new rights to those of 1789. For example:
- "The law guarantees women equal rights to men in all areas";
- everyone has the right to work and to defend their rights through trade unions and strikes;
- the nation guarantees everyone, especially children, mothers and elderly workers, health care and rest;
- children and adults have equal access to education, which is free and secular;
- the right of asylum for people persecuted in their own country.

This preamble is still part of the French Constitution today!

In 1945, [Social Security] is created: it pays for everyone's health care, pensions for elderly people and help for families. Everyone contributes according to their means and receives according to their needs: a fine example of fraternity.

But the Fourth Republic has a problem: governments change very often, sometimes after only a few months. The country struggles to make decisions, especially during the Algerian War, which begins in 1954.

In 1958, in the middle of a crisis, General de Gaulle is called back...

In 1958, France is going through a serious crisis because of the Algerian War. General de Gaulle, the leader of Free France during the Second World War, returns to power.

He has a new [Constitution] written, giving more power to the President of the Republic so that the state is more stable. On 28 September 1958, the French approve it by referendum, with almost 80% of the vote. It comes into force on 4 October 1958: the Fifth Republic is born.

It is still France's Republic today!

The 1958 Constitution says France is "an indivisible, secular, democratic and social Republic". It includes:
- the motto "Liberty, Equality, Fraternity";
- the emblem: the blue, white and red flag;
- the anthem: La Marseillaise;
- the principle: "government of the people, by the people and for the people".

It organises the powers:
- the President of the Republic, head of state;
- the government, led by the Prime Minister;
- Parliament, made up of the National Assembly and the Senate, which votes the laws;
- the Constitutional Council, which checks that laws respect the Constitution.

Charles de Gaulle becomes the first President of the Fifth Republic in January 1959.

The Constitution has been changed several times since, but it has lasted more than 65 years. Only the Third Republic lasted longer!

Children are citizens too, with rights! But for a long time, many children around the world worked in factories or fields, didn't go to school, or were mistreated.

On 20 November 1989, the United Nations adopts the [Convention on the Rights of the Child]. It is a text signed by almost every country in the world. France ratified it in 1990.

It says that every child up to the age of 18 has rights, whatever their country, skin colour or religion, whether a girl or a boy. For example:
- the right to a name and a nationality;
- the right to go to school;
- the right to health care and food;
- the right to be protected from violence, abuse and exploitation;
- the right not to fight in wars and not to work too young;
- the right to play and have leisure time;
- the right to give their opinion on matters that concern them;
- the right to protection if they are a refugee or have a disability.

Every year, 20 November is International Children's Day.

In France, a children's ombudsman helps children whose rights are not respected. There is also a free phone number, 119, for children in danger or who need help.

Having rights also means respecting other children's rights.

Being a [citizen] means having rights, but also duties towards others and towards the Republic.

A citizen's rights include:
- freedom of expression, of conscience and of assembly;
- equality before the law;
- the right to vote and to be elected, from age 18;
- the right to education, health care and protection.

A citizen's duties include:
- respecting the law and other people;
- paying taxes according to your means: they pay for schools, hospitals, roads, the police, firefighters...;
- taking part in the country's defence: today, young people must register for the census at 16 and attend the Defence and Citizenship Day;
- serving on a [jury] if you are chosen by lot, to judge the most serious crimes;
- helping a person in danger.

Voting is a right, but it is also a [civic duty]. In France, voting is not compulsory, unlike in Belgium or Australia. But when you don't vote, you let others decide for you. When many voters stay at home, this is called abstention.

Fraternity is also a duty: showing solidarity, helping the weakest, respecting differences.

Remember article 4 of the 1789 Declaration: "Liberty consists in being able to do anything that does not harm others." My freedom ends where other people's freedom begins!

You're not 18 yet, so you can't vote... but you can already act like a citizen!

At school, you can:
- elect [class representatives] or be one: they represent the pupils, pass on their ideas and help solve problems;
- take part in the pupils' council;
- respect the school rules, which set out everyone's rights and duties;
- help a classmate who is struggling, refuse bullying and respect differences.

In your town, there is often a [children's town council] or youth council. Children are elected to it by other children. They suggest projects for their town or village: a skate park, a cycle path, a festival, an action for the environment... The first children's town council in France was created in 1979, in Schiltigheim, in Alsace.

You can also:
- take part in a club or association (sport, culture, helping others...);
- sort your rubbish and protect the environment;
- find out about things, listen to different opinions and debate without quarrelling;
- go with your parents to the polling station to see how people vote.

The symbols of the Republic are all around you: the flag and the motto on your school, Marianne at the town hall, La Marseillaise that you learn in class, 14 July.

The Republic took more than a hundred years to settle in. Freedoms and rights were won little by little, thanks to the courage of many women and men. It is up to each of us to keep them alive!
